    "data": {
      "A": "Research a serial, cache-conscious MSD radix replacement for the insertion-sort kernel in src/bucketsort.c. Sort indices into the existing NUL-terminated string storage using unsigned-byte histograms, counted prefix offsets, and reusable scatter space. Treat NUL as terminal and never inspect uninitialized record padding. Use an iterative worklist and insertion sort only for small partitions, with a general workload-based cutoff. Preserve strcmp ordering, duplicate multiplicity, and original token spelling; do not convert strings to numeric keys. Derive bucket capacities from actual counts rather than N/nbuckets, so skewed distributions are safe. Produce a complete candidate and a separable sorting kernel, documenting auxiliary memory and unchanged-harness correctness and timing.",
      "B": "Research an independent comparison-sort alternative: bottom-up merge sorting of indices, followed by a bounded parallel version that sorts up to four disjoint runs and merges them. All comparisons must preserve strcmp semantics; strings remain read-only and merge destinations must be race-free. Compare serial execution with supported two- and four-worker configurations, including worker startup and merging in end-to-end measurements. Use only threading facilities available under the unchanged compiler and build configuration; if none are usable, retain the serial merge-sort investigation. Explore general size-based serial/parallel dispatch, not input-identity detection. Avoid assumptions about balanced first-character buckets, numeric ordering, or distinct keys. Deliver the best validated standalone program and clearly separated sorting/parallel components.",
      "C": "Research input/output overhead independently of the specialized sorting trees. Implement block-buffered fread tokenization and batched fwrite emission, retaining the fixed-stride, NUL-terminated string representation and index-based sorting interface for integration. Parse all four header fields correctly, including the source's length and bucket-count increment conventions. Handle whitespace across buffer boundaries, CRLF, and EOF immediately after the final token. Preserve token spelling, leading zeros, duplicates, and exactly one output newline per token; never normalize data through integer conversion. Use size-appropriate counters and checked allocation arithmetic. Use standard qsort with strcmp ordering as a general sorting scaffold. Where the existing budget permits, compare buffered and original-style I/O around the same scaffold to distinguish I/O gains from algorithm replacement.",
      "G": "Integrate only components delivered by A/B/C, preserving the unchanged tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, and permissions. Retain the fastest validated standalone candidate as a fallback. Prioritize combining C's parser/writer with the better measured sorting implementation from A or B; adapt interfaces carefully rather than mechanically merging programs. Evaluate each proposed integration as a new complete candidate: component gains are not additive, and threading, buffering, extra allocations, or cache pressure can make the combination slower. Use the existing harness's correctness checks and end-to-end mean-time metric within its existing evaluation budget. Audit lexical ordering, NUL handling, token preservation, skewed bucket occupancy, index bounds, memory use, and parallel synchronization. Select the fastest fully validated measured program, including a standalone program if integration loses.",
      "analysis": "The supplied source is Problem D; A/B/C are research-role names, not the other contest problems. The baseline passed four automatic cases and averaged 0.417086 seconds, with samples spanning 0.414601–0.419341 seconds. There is no phase profile or documented workload distribution, so bottleneck attribution remains a hypothesis. Structurally, per-bucket insertion sorting can require quadratic numbers of strcmp calls, while per-token fscanf/printf introduces another plausible cost. Equal-sized bucket slices are not justified by the statement and should be replaced by count-derived boundaries. Preserve the reference's lexical string ordering and exact token text, not an assumed numeric interpretation. The target has four ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU; parallel overhead matters at this runtime. All research must preserve correctness for every valid input and use only the unchanged experimental environment."
